\n  1419→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1420→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1421→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1422→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1423→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1424→    },\n  1425→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1426→      \"plot_summary\": \"In the face of a merciless plague known as the Red Death—marked by bleeding pores and swift, gruesome death—Prince Prospero retreats with a thousand courtiers into a secluded, heavily fortified abbey, seeking refuge from the spreading horror. The abbey, a marvel of opulent design, features seven distinct chambers, each adorned in a different color and illuminated by stained-glass windows, except for the westernmost chamber, shrouded in black velvet and blood-red glass, which evokes an unsettling dread. Within this sanctuary, the revelers indulge in a lavish masked ball, their merriment a defiant act against the grim reality outside. The atmosphere is one of extravagant distraction, yet it is punctuated by the ominous chimes of an ebony clock in the black chamber, which tolls each hour, freezing the festivities and reminding all of the inevitability of time and death.\\n\\nAs the night unfolds, the revelers grow increasingly uneasy, their joy fraying under the weight of the clock’s chilling tolls. At midnight, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, with a blood-stained visage and a corpse-like mask—moving silently through the colored rooms. The intrusion shatters the illusion of safety, and Prince Prospero, enraged by the violation of his sanctuary, pursues the figure with a dagger, driven by a mix of fury and denial. The chase leads them into the black chamber, where the figure turns, revealing the mask to be empty. In that moment, the prince collapses dead, a final confirmation of the futility of his attempt to escape fate.\\n\\nThe revelers, now realizing the horrifying truth, rush into the black chamber only to find the mask empty and the figure vanished—yet the Red Death has already infiltrated their midst. One by one, they fall, succumbing to the very plague they believed they had escaped. The ebony clock chimes once more, then falls silent, as darkness consumes the abbey. The story ends not with triumph or redemption, but with the chilling realization that no fortress, no wealth, no isolation can shield one from the universal fate of mortality. The prince’s ambition to defy death through isolation and indulgence is utterly undone by the inescapable nature of the plague and the symbolic weight of the black chamber, which represents the inevitability of death.\\n\\nThe narrative underscores the tragic irony of human denial in the face of universal truth. Prince Prospero, once a symbol of power and privilege, becomes a cautionary figure whose arrogance leads to his downfall. The courtiers, bound by their shared delusion of safety, are equally complicit in their demise. The story’s progression—from the initial refuge to the final collapse—reveals a world where spectacle masks despair and where the most elaborate defenses are rendered meaningless by the unseen, unstoppable force of death.\",\n  1427→      \"themes\": [\n  1428→        \"identity\",\n  1429→        \"ambition\",\n  1430→        \"loss\"\n  1431→      ],\n  1432→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1433→    },\n  1434→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1435→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1436→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1437→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1438→      },\n  1439→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1440→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1441→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1442→      },\n  1443→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1444→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1445→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1446→      },\n  1447→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1448→        \"model\": \"qwen3:3